          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In research mathematics, when one says
            = succeed "," do
              %em analytic geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+functions"} analytic functions
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+expansion"} Taylor expansion
            and by
            %strong analytic geometry
            one usually means the study of geometry of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifolds"} complex manifolds
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+analytic+spaces"} complex analytic spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+domains"} Stein domains
            and related notions. More generally one may replace the complex numbers by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-archimedean+fields"} non-archimedean fields
            in which case one speaks of
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
          %p
            Similarly to an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} algebraic variety
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+variety"} analytic variety
            is locally given as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero+locus"} zero locus
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+set"} finite set
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+functions"} analytic functions
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+functions"} holomorphic functions
            in complex analytic geometry.
          %p
            A short survey can be found in a chapter of Dieudonne’s
            = succeed "." do
              %em Panorama of pure mathematics
          %p
            In addition to analytic geometry over complex numbers, there is also another formalism which allows for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/archimedean+field"} nonarchimedean
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+fields"} ground fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/schemes"} schemes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bercovich+spectrum"} Bercovich spectra
            of certain commutative Banach algebras, so-called
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affinoid"} affinoid
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+topology"} Grothendieck topology
            = succeed ".)" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+space"} analytic space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal geometry
            and has its main applications in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/arithmetic+geometry"} arithmetic geometry
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representation+theory"} representation theory
          %p
            It is an open problem to find an appropriate analogue of rigid analytic geometry in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+geometry"} noncommutative geometry
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mirror+symmetry"} mirror symmetry
          %p
            Local properties of analytic manifolds and spaces are studied in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+analytic+geometry"} local analytic geometry
          %h2#theorems Theorems
          %p (…)
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ostrowski%27s+theorem"} Ostrowski's theorem
          %p (…)
          %h2#holomorphic_functions_of_several_complex_variables Holomorphic functions of several complex variables
          %p
            This section is about certain aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+functions"} holomorphic functions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n \to \mathbb{C}
          %p
            Currently it concentrates on aspects of relevance in the application to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edge-of-the-wedge_theorem"} edge-of-the-wedge
            theorem.
          %p
            From the viewpoint of complex
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %em local
            theory that describes the situation in coordinate patches.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Several_complex_variables"} Wikipedia
          %p
            When we talk about holomorphic functions in the following and do not specify the domain, we will always assume that the domain is an open, simply connected subset of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
          %h3#relevance_to_quantum_field_theory_qft relevance to quantum field theory (QFT)
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            formulation (actually the following description is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
            of quantum mechanics in a nutshell)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/selfadjoint+operator"} selfadjoint operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{H}
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            of a physical system, while normed vectors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℋ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in \mathcal{H}
            represent the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/states"} states
            the system can be in. The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+number"} real number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi A
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle y, A x \rangle
            represents the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/probability"} probability
            that a system starting in state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            will be in state
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            after a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/measurement"} measurement
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            we often encounter a set of operators indexed by several complex variables
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi z
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi z
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = (z_1, z_2, ...)
            and try to deduce properties of the theory from the function
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(z) := \langle y, A(z)x \rangle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+functions"} holomorphic functions
            of several variables is promoted to an irreplaceable tool in quantum field theory.
          %h3#hartogs_theorem Hartogs’ theorem
          %p
            One striking difference of functions of several
            %em real
            variables and several
            %em complex
            variables is described by Hartogs’ theorem on separate analyticity:
          %ul
            %li
              Theorem (Hartogs): Let f be a
              = succeed "-valued" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ⊂
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset \mathbb{C}^n
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_j
              when the other coordinates
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi z
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z_k
              are fixed. Then f is analytic as a function of all n coordinates.
          %p
            %em> remark
            \: There are no other assumptions about f necessary, it needs not to be continous or even measurable. Note that in the real case the property of being partially differentiable alone encodes nearly no information about a function.
          %p Reference:
          %ul
            %li
              Paul Garrett:
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.umn.edu/~garrett/m/complex/hartogs.pdf"} Hartogs theorem
          %p
            %em> relevance
            \: When reading
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            literature you will often encounter the claim that given functions are holomorphic, Hartogs’ theorem simplifies the task of checking these claims considerably, because you have to check the holomorphy in every single variable only.
          %h3#analogues_from_the_onedimensional_theory analogues from the one-dimensional theory
          %p Some results remain true in the multi dimensional case.
          %ul
            %li Identity theorem: If a holomorphic function is zero in a neighbourhood of a point, it is the zero function.
          %p
            %em> remark
            \: As usual the domain is supposed to be an open, simply connected (not necessarily proper) subset of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
          %h3#domains_of_holomorphy domains of holomorphy
          %p
            One of the most notably difference of the theory of
            %em one
            complex variable and of
            %em several
            complex variables is that the
            %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Riemann_mapping_theorem"} riemann mapping theorem
            fails in several complex variables, which is in a certain sense the reason why in several complex variables there are domains which can be enlarged such that
            %em all
            holomorphic functions extend to the larger domain.
          %p
            %em handwaving
            why this is not possible in one dimension: According to the riemann mapping theorem every domain (open, simply connected proper subset of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi E
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E: = \{ z: |z| \lt 1 \}
          %p
            Some domains in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
            %em do
            have the property that they cannot be enlarged, and since this is an interesting property, the name
            %strong domain of holomorphy
            was coined for these, and the question how they could be described was promoted to an interesting research topic in the beginning of the 20th century.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Domain_of_holomorphy"} Wikipedia
          %h4#edge_of_the_wedge_theorems edge of the wedge theorems
          %p These theorems desribe situations where holomorphic functions defined on specific domains (the wedges) can be continued to holomorphic functions of larger domains.
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edge-of-the-wedge_theorem"} Wikipedia
          %p
            They are a valuable tool in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AQFT"} AQFT
            (and were in fact discovered by one of the fathers of the theory,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nikolay_Bogoliubov"} Nikolay Bogolyubov
          %p We state here one version that will be of use to the nLab:
          %ul
            %li
              theorem (edge of the wedge): Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi z
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi r
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K := \{ z \in \mathbb{C}^n: |z| \le r \}
              be a ball in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^n
              and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                    %mo ⊂
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} \subset \mathbb{R}^n
              be an open convex cone such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo ∩
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ≠
                      %mi ∅
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} \cap (- \mathcal{C}) \neq \emptyset
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi z
                    %mo =
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi iy
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z = x + iy
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in \mathbb{R}^n
              and define an open domain G by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi z
                      %mo =
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi iy
                      %mo :
                      %mi z
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi K
                      %mo ,
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G:= \{ z = x + iy: z \in K, y \in \mathcal{C} \}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              be a holomorphic function in G and assume that
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mrow
                          %mi y
                          %mo →
                          %mn 0
                          %mo ,
                          %mi y
                          %mo ∈
                          %mi 𝒞
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo +
                      %mi iy
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\lim_{y \to 0, y \in \mathcal{C}} f(x + iy)
              %p
                exists for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi B
                          %mi r
                        %mo ⊂
                        %msup
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in B_r \subset \mathbb{R}^n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi B
                        %mi r
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B_r
                is an open ball with radius r. (The limit may not depend on the specific sequence chosen). Then
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is holomorph extendable into an open region
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                      %mo ∪
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \cup G_0
                with
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi G
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⋃
                        %mrow
                          %mi x
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi B
                            %mi r
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mi z
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi z
                      %mo −
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi θ
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mi dist
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo ,
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      G_0 := \bigcup_{x \in B_r} \{ z: |z-x| \le \theta \cdot dist(x, \partial U) \}
              %p
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mi θ
                      %mo &lt;
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \lt \theta \lt 1
                a constant that is independent from
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ,
                        %msub
                          %mi B
                          %mi r
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, B_r
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          %p
            %em> proof
            \: V.S.Vladimirov, “theory of functions of several complex variables” (
            %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0125.31904&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
            )
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/several+complex+variables"}> several complex variables
                ,
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"}> rigid analytic geometry
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+spectrum"} analytic spectrum
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+space"} analytic space
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stein+space"} Stein space
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berkovich+space"} Berkovich space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+analytic+geometry"} additive analytic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-topology"} G-topology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oka+principle"} Oka principle
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+geometry"} p-adic geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B1-homotopy+theory"} B1-homotopy theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"} global analytic geometry
              %ul
                %li
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/overconvergent+global+analytic+geometry"} overconvergent global analytic geometry
          %h2#References References
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li#Neeman07
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Amnon+Neeman"}> Amnon Neeman
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Algebraic and analytic geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %strong 345
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.cambridge.org/us/academic/subjects/mathematics/geometry-and-topology/algebraic-and-analytic-geometry"} ISBN:9780521709835
          %p
            Course notes on (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/global+analytic+geometry"}> global
            ) analytic geometry are in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9d%C3%A9ric+Paugam"}> Frédéric Paugam
              ,
              %em Global analytic geometry and the functional equation
              (2010) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~fpaugam/documents/enseignement/master-global-analytic-geometry.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Kiran Sridhara Kedlaya,
                %em Introduction to Rigid Analytic Geometry
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www-math.mit.edu/~kedlaya/18.727/notes.html"}> web
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Conrad"}> Brian Conrad
                ,
                %em Several approaches to non-archimedean geometry
                (
                %a{:href => "http://math.stanford.edu/~conrad/papers/aws.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            A gentle and modern introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+manifolds"} complex manifolds
            that starts with an extensive exposition of the local theory is this:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Huybrechts"}> Daniel Huybrechts
                ,
                %em Complex geometry. An introduction.
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:1055.14001&format=complete"}> ZMATH entry
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Grauert"}> Hans Grauert
                , Reinhold Remmert,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Theory of Stein spaces
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 236
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Coherent analytic sheaves
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 265
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Komplexe Räume
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 136
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1007/BF01362011"} DOI
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Berkovich+space"} Berkovich space
            analytic geometry as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+geometry"} algebraic geometry
            in the general sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+To%C3%ABn"} Bertrand Toën
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Vezzosi"} Gabriele Vezzosi
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Oren+Ben-Bassat"}> Oren Ben-Bassat
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kobi+Kremnizer"} Kobi Kremnizer
              %em Non-Archimedean analytic geometry as relative algebraic geometry
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1312.0338"}> arXiv:1312.0338
              )
          %p
            For more see the references at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rigid+analytic+geometry"} rigid analytic geometry
            and at
